### 🐞 TWO SILENT-FAILURE BUGS FOUND BY AUDITING THE GENERATED CSS
- [x] **`duration-fast` and friends compiled to nothing.** Tailwind v4 has an `--ease-*` theme
      namespace but **no `--duration-*`**, so mapping them in `@theme` is silently dropped — every
      transition in the app was running at the browser default. Fixed with explicit `@utility`
      rules in `base.css` that set both `transition-duration` **and** `--tw-duration` (tw-animate-css
      reads the latter). Neither typecheck, lint nor build reports this; only diffing the emitted
      CSS does.
- [x] **`[&>*:first-child]:` variants were dropped entirely.** Tailwind v4's scanner treats `>` as a
      delimiter inside JSX, so the table's signature leading threshold bar never rendered. Moved to
      a real `.hs-row-threshold` rule in `base.css`, driven by `data-threshold`.

### ✅ SPEC DEFECT — found, corrected in code, then corrected in the doc (closed 2026-07-28)
- [x] **`15_UI_SPEC_v1.md` §4.3 named the wrong locale.** It asked for lakh/crore grouping
      "via `Intl.NumberFormat('en-PK')`" while printing `PKR 12,84,500` in its own §5 sketch.
      Measured on Node 20: `en-PK` → `1,284,500`, and its currency form renders the symbol as
      **`Rs`**, which the same section explicitly bans; `en-IN` → `12,84,500`. §4.3 now says
      `en-IN` and carries a note with the measurement so the next reader does not "fix" it back.
      The same bullet's `fmtPKR()` reference went with it — that helper has **never existed in
      this repo**; it is a rule inherited from the HOSTIX-APP Electron codebase, where the
      formatter did emit a symbol. Here it does not, so "never pair it with a `pkr` span" was not
      merely stale but the exact inverse of what §4.3's symbol-treatment bullet requires. §17's
      Definition of Done updated to match.
- [x] **`13_PRODUCTION_READINESS.md` §6.7 rewritten** — it carried an `fmtPkr`/`fmtPkrShort` sample
      attributed to `packages/db/src/formatters.ts`. **Neither function has ever existed**; that
      file holds `fmtCnic` + `fmtPhone` only. The sample was wrong three ways: `en-PK` does not
      group the Pakistani way (its own comment claimed `→ PKR 1,00,000`; the real output is
      `100,000`), `style:'currency'` renders `Rs` which §4.3 bans, and it abbreviated above 1 lakh
      in KPI cards where §4.3 permits abbreviation only above 10 lakh and only in chart axes.
      Section now documents `apps/web/src/lib/format.ts` as it actually is.
      **Checked while there — no server-side currency formatting exists anywhere.** The only
      `en-PK` left in code is `payments.ts:649`, a *month* label (`toLocaleString` with
      month/year), which is correct for dates.

### ✅ RECEIPTS — pdf-receipts worker deleted, replaced with on-demand rendering (2026-07-28)
- [x] **The worker was a stub that reported success, and it could never have run.** It SELECTed
      six columns that are not on the schema (`p.amount_paid` → is `paid`, `p.month_label` → is
      `month`, `p.notes`, `s.full_name` → is `name`, and `receipt_generated`/`receipt_generated_at`
      in its UPDATE, which no migration creates), so it would have thrown on first execution.
      It never did, because **nothing enqueued it** (see below). Meanwhile it set a
      `receipt_generated` flag, so the DB recorded receipts that did not exist.
- [x] **Replaced rather than repaired: `GET /payments/:id/receipt` renders and streams the PDF.**
      Founder chose on-demand over the specced async-to-storage pipeline. The reason the stored
      shape is wrong here: payments can be edited and voided, so a PDF written at creation time is
      a snapshot that starts lying the moment either happens and then circulates as proof of a
      payment that no longer stands. On-demand also removes a storage bucket + per-tenant path
      policy, retention/cleanup, signed-URL expiry, and the lost-job failure mode.
      Built with `pdfkit` (built-in Helvetica/Courier — no font files to ship). A voided payment
      still returns its receipt, carrying a VOID stamp and a footer stating it is not proof of
      payment. Cross-tenant id → 404, indistinguishable from missing.
- [x] **`fmtPkr()` added to `packages/db/src/formatters.ts`** for documents, with
      `formatters.test.ts` (12 tests) asserting the exact lakh-grouped output. That test is the
      drift guard between it and `apps/web/src/lib/format.ts`, which must implement the same rule
      independently because the web app cannot import a package that pulls in `pg`.
- [x] **Verified by rendering**, not by typecheck: four cases (paid / partial / void / all-optional
      -fields-null) written to real PDFs, content streams inflated and the text operators read back
      to confirm the figures. That caught a bug typecheck cannot see — the concession line used
      U+2212 MINUS SIGN, which is absent from PDFKit's WinAnsi-encoded built-in Helvetica and
      rendered as `"`, so the line read `" PKR 2,500`. Em dashes replaced with words for the same
      reason.
- [x] API spec Module 4 amended: `receiptStatus` and `receiptUrl` are gone, the new endpoint is
      documented, and the design change is explained inline.
      ⚠️ `POST /payments/:id/send-receipt` still lists `receiptUrl` — that endpoint is unbuilt and
      belongs to the deferred WhatsApp work; it needs the same treatment when it lands.

### 🐞 WORKER INVESTIGATION (2026-07-28, founder-requested) — NOTHING TRIGGERS ANY WORKER
**`apps/api` contains no BullMQ producer at all.** Only `new Worker(...)` is ever constructed —
there is no `new Queue(...)`, no `.add(...)`, no repeatable/cron job registration anywhere in the
repo, and the three GitHub workflows (`ci`, `staging-smoke`, `uptime`) do not invoke jobs either.
All four remaining workers connect to Redis, subscribe, and wait forever on queues nobody writes
to. Reported, not fixed — decide the shape first.

| Worker | Is the work happening? | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `rent-generate` | **Yes, but not via the worker.** | `POST /payments/generate-monthly` does the same job inline, tenant-scoped through `withTenant`, owner-only. The worker is a **second, divergent implementation** of the same logic, unreachable. Monthly rent is therefore manual — someone must call the endpoint. |
| `auto-cancel` | **No.** | Pending cancellations past their date are never confirmed. |
| `billing-sync` | **No.** | Subscription/plan state (trial→active→expired→suspended) never syncs. |
| `email-send` | **No.** | No email is ever sent by the system. |

- [x] 🐞 **`auto-cancel.ts` had the same broken-SQL defect pdf-receipts had — FIXED 2026-07-28.**
      Four non-existent columns across the handler: `cancellations.cancellation_date` (the column
      is `vacate_date`), `cancellations.auto_confirmed`, and `beds.occupant_id` — the beds table
      has no such column, the link runs the other way (`students.bed_id`) — plus it wrote
      `beds.status = 'available'`, which fails that table's CHECK (`vacant`|`occupied`|
      `maintenance`).
      ⚠️ **Correction to this file's earlier claim.** A previous note here said triggering the
      worker "today would half-apply a cancellation". That was wrong: the *first* statement
      referenced `cancellation_date` and `auto_confirmed`, so it threw before writing anything and
      nothing was applied. The half-application risk was latent, not live — it would have appeared
      the moment someone fixed statement 1 without adding a transaction, which is precisely what
      made the transaction worth adding alongside the SQL fix.
      Rewritten to mirror `POST /cancellations/:id/confirm` exactly — same three writes, same audit
      row — because a worker that vacates a student differently is a second definition of
      "cancelled" and the two would drift. Additions on top of the old behaviour: an
      `audit_log` row (INVARIANT-5; automatic vacations were the only untraceable ones), a
      per-cancellation transaction so one bad row cannot roll back another hostel's, a
      claim-by-`UPDATE ... WHERE status='pending'` that makes the sweep idempotent and safe against
      a warden confirming by hand mid-run, and a date comparison in the *hostel's* timezone rather
      than the container's UTC. Also dropped the old worker's `students.deleted_at = NOW()`, which
      the route does not do — soft-deleting a vacated student would hide them from every list.
      `confirmed_by IS NULL` on a confirmed row is now the marker of an automatic confirmation.
      Covered by `apps/api/src/__tests__/auto-cancel.test.ts` (7 tests): bed actually released,
      audit row written, future dates untouched, idempotent on re-run, multi-tenant sweep scoped
      per hostel, already-confirmed rows ignored, and a student with no bed assigned.
- [ ] **Decide how scheduled work runs** before wiring any producer: BullMQ repeatable jobs
      registered at boot (simple, but every API replica registers them — needs a single-owner
      guard), or an external scheduler hitting authenticated endpoints (visible in logs, easier to
      run manually, matches how `generate-monthly` already works). The second matches the grain of
      what is already built.
- [ ] **Decide `rent-generate`'s fate** — delete the worker and keep the endpoint as the one
      implementation, or move the endpoint to enqueue. Two copies of the billing rule is the real
      risk here; they can already disagree.

