      `display_name, theme, language, totp_secret_enc, totp_backup_codes` — added the columns
      (renamed plaintext `totp_secret`→`totp_secret_enc`). Also caught `pdf-receipts.ts`
      SELECTing `h.tagline` (same class of runtime crash) → added `hostels.tagline`.
      ⚠️ Migration 009 must be applied to any live DB.
- [x] PARTIAL 2026-07-22: role 'super_admin' (not in users CHECK) removed from the 6
      `requireRole` guards in students.ts — was a dead literal that never matched. NOTE: those
      GET/POST/PATCH student routes still exclude `chain_manager` while import/reveal-cnic
      include it — an authorization-policy inconsistency for the owner to decide (add
      chain_manager to student read/write? left unchanged).
- [x] students.ts JSON-Schema validation — **DONE 2026-07-26** (`d892d11`, 7 tests green in CI).
      Params/querystring/body schemas on every route. Fixed three 500s-that-should-be-400s
      (`/students/not-a-uuid` → uuid cast failure; `?limit=abc` → `LIMIT NaN`; PATCH `status` →
      CHECK violation) and one guard wrong the other way: `!monthly_fee` rejected a fee of 0, so a
      scholarship/free bed could not be recorded — column is NUMERIC(10,2) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0.
      Status enum mirrors migration 002's CHECK. No `maximum` on limit on purpose: the handler
      already clamps to 100, and a 400 there would be a silent contract change for callers.
- [ ] DEFERRED (no code consumer yet — not adding dead schema): warden flags
      (`can_edit/can_delete/can_settings`) for DELETE-gating; hostel branding fields
      (`brand_color/show_branding/auto_month_advance`). Add with the feature that uses them.
- [x] **`payments.notes` accepted and silently discarded — FIXED 2026-07-28 (session 12)**, taking
      option (a): migration `013_payments_notes.sql` adds the column, and both handlers persist it.
      Create stores and echoes it, `GET /payments/:id` returns it, owner `PATCH` edits it. The
      UPDATE uses COALESCE so an unrelated edit cannot blank a warden's note — that failure mode
      has its own test. Capped at 1000 chars in the JSON Schema rather than by a VARCHAR(n), so an
      over-long note is a 400 with a message instead of a constraint violation and a 500. Not
      returned by `GET /payments`: free text in a ledger table is truncated to uselessness in every
      row. Audit rows carry the note on create and edit (INVARIANT-5).
      `payment-notes.test.ts` (6 tests) — a schema-only promise is invisible to typecheck, lint and
      every existing test, because nothing fails; only reading the value back catches it.
      ⚠️ **Migration 013 must be applied to staging + production.**
- [ ] **Two more phantom `notes` fields of the same class** — found 2026-07-28 while fixing the
      one above, by grepping every `notes` in the routes against the migrations.
      **(a) `POST /rooms/shift`** (`rooms.ts:335`) declares `notes` and destructures it as `_notes`
      — accepted, explicitly discarded. Unlike payments this should probably NOT become a column on
      `rooms`: the note is about the *move*, so it belongs on the transfer/audit record or nowhere.
      **(b) `expenses`** — `05_API_SPECIFICATION.md` shows `notes` in both the list response and
      the create body, but `expenses.ts` never mentions the field and no migration creates the
      column, so the spec documents something the route will not even accept. Decide per case:
      persist, or drop from the contract. Neither is urgent; both are logged so the next reader
      does not assume they work.
      <details><summary>original report</summary>
      found 2026-07-27 while building the
      web payment form. Both `POST /payments` (payments.ts:109) and `PATCH /payments/:id`
      (payments.ts:401) declare `notes` in their JSON Schema, but migration 003 defines no `notes`
      column and neither handler references one — the INSERT column list stops at `created_by`. A
      client can send a note, receive 201, and have it vanish. This is the same defect class as the
      `extra_charges` bug fixed in §0 (in the schema, never persisted), which is why it is worth
      fixing rather than tolerating. Two honest options: **(a)** add the column in a migration and
      persist it on create + PATCH, or **(b)** drop `notes` from both schemas so the contract stops
      promising it. (a) is the more useful one — a warden recording "paid in two instalments, second
      by JazzCash" has nowhere else to put it. Deliberately NOT fixed in the frontend slice: it is
      an API + live-schema change, not a UI one. `apps/web` omits the field until this is resolved.
      </details>
- [x] **`GET /payments/defaulters` omits `paymentId` — FIXED 2026-07-28 (session 12).**
      `p.id as "paymentId"` added to the SELECT; `defaulters.test.ts` (3 tests) asserts the whole
      chain rather than the field alone — the id comes back, a POST for the same month really does
      409 `PAY_DUPLICATE_MONTH` (which is *why* the id is needed), and PATCHing that id settles the
      row and drops it from the list. API spec Module 4 updated; while there, `paymentRiskScore`
      and `whatsappMessage` were removed from the documented response — both were specified and
      never built, and the WhatsApp work they belong to is deferred. `apps/web`'s `Defaulter` type
      now carries `paymentId`. The rows still link to the student, because the remaining blocker
      moved: the payment-edit UI does not exist yet.
      <details><summary>original report</summary>
      found 2026-07-28 while building the
      defaulters screen (payments.ts:263-281). The endpoint returns `studentId` but not the id of
      the payment row that is actually outstanding, which blocks the screen's whole purpose: every
      defaulter *already has* a payment row for that month (the query filters on status
      pending/partial), so settling one is `PATCH /payments/:id`, never `POST /payments` — a POST
      hits the duplicate-month guard (payments.ts:145-154) and 409s on every row. With no payment
      id in the response there is nothing to deep-link a settle action to, so `apps/web` ships the
      list with rows linking to the student instead of a "collect" button that could not work.
      FIX: add `p.id as "paymentId"` to the SELECT. One line, no schema change, unblocks the
      collect flow. Pairs with the still-unbuilt payment-edit UI (`PATCH /payments/:id`).
      </details>

